#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111823 :tutorial/content "The **power of quotient rule** states that if we want to raise the quotient\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{y}\n$$\n\nto some power $n$, we must raise the numerator and denominator to the power $n$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y}\\right)^n=\\dfrac{x^n}{y^n}\n$$\n\nFor example,\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{x}{y}\\right)^4=\\dfrac{x^4}{y^4}\n$$\n\nTo see why the power of quotient rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again the long way. We get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(\\frac{x}{y})^{4} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{x}{y} \\cdot \\frac{x}{y} \\cdot \\frac{x}{y} \\cdot \\frac{x}{y} &=  \\\\\n\\frac{x \\cdot x \\cdot x \\cdot x}{y \\cdot y \\cdot y \\cdot y} &=  \\\\\n&= \\frac{x^{4}}{y^{4}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe power of quotient rule is just a shortcut for the process above." :tutorial/id #uuid "9aedc7af-544c-5314-a986-2cd6ce64479b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1484 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
